.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt PRLIMIT64 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm prlimit64 ,
.Nm getrlimit ,
.Nm setrlimit ,
.Nm ugetrlimit
.Nd get or set process resource limits
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Fd #define _GNU_SOURCE
.In sys/resource.h
.Ft int
.Fn getrlimit "int resource" "struct rlimit *rlim"
.Ft int
.Fn setrlimit "int resource" "const struct rlimit *rlim"
.Ft int
.Fn prlimit "pid_t pid" "int resource" "const struct rlimit *new_limit" "struct rlimit *old_limit"
.Fd #define prlimit64 prlimit
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn getrlimit
reads a resource's limits for the calling process.
.Fn setrlimit
sets them.
Each limit has a soft value,
.Va rlim_cur ,
and a hard value,
.Va rlim_max ,
in
.Vt struct rlimit .
Musl declares both fields as the 64-bit type
.Vt rlim_t .
.Dv RLIM_INFINITY
denotes no limit.
.Pp
With
.Dv _GNU_SOURCE
defined, musl declares
.Fn prlimit
and defines
.Nm prlimit64
as a macro for it.
.Fa pid
selects the process; zero selects the caller.
Another process may be selected by a caller with effective user ID 0
or the same real user ID as that process.
If
.Fa new_limit
is non-null, it supplies new values.
If
.Fa old_limit
is non-null, it receives the values from before the attempted change.
Either or both pointers may be null.
.Pp
Three resources are stored and enforced:
.Bl -tag -width RLIMIT_NOFILE
.It Dv RLIMIT_NPROC
The number of processes with the same real user ID across the machine.
Fork returns
.Er EAGAIN
when the parent's soft limit is reached.
Real user ID 0 is exempt.
A change of real user ID moves the process to the new user's count.
.It Dv RLIMIT_NOFILE
One greater than the highest descriptor number the process may receive.
The hard limit cannot exceed 1024, even for root.
Open returns
.Er EMFILE
when no descriptor below the soft limit is free.
Dup2 returns
.Er EBADF
for a destination at or above the soft limit.
.It Dv RLIMIT_AS
The bytes covered by the process's virtual memory regions.
Growth beyond the soft limit makes mmap return
.Er ENOMEM ,
leaves the break unchanged in brk, and prevents stack growth.
.El
.Pp
For these resources the soft limit must not exceed the hard limit.
A process may lower its hard limits; only a caller with effective user
ID 0 may raise a hard limit.
Fork inherits both values and exec preserves them.
The kernel's initial soft and hard limits are 4096 processes,
1024 descriptors, and unlimited address space.
Init may replace these defaults as described in
.Xr limits 7 .
.Pp
.Dv RLIMIT_STACK
always reports 8388608 bytes for both values.
Every other resource number, including an unknown number, reports
unlimited values.
Setting any resource other than the three stored resources succeeds
without changing anything, even if its soft value exceeds its hard value.
.Pp
The kernel stores limits in 32 bits.
A 64-bit value with a nonzero high word, or the value 4294967295,
is treated as unlimited.
An unlimited value is returned with all 64 bits set.
.Pp
The i386 system call
.Nm prlimit64
is number 340.
Musl uses it for
.Fn prlimit ,
.Fn getrlimit ,
and
.Fn setrlimit .
The older calls
.Nm getrlimit
(76),
.Nm setrlimit
(75), and
.Nm ugetrlimit
(191) use two unsigned 32-bit fields, with 4294967295 as infinity.
Musl has no separate
.Fn ugetrlimit
declaration; its getrlimit fallback uses that kernel number on i386.
.Pp
The kernel copies new values in, records the previous values, attempts
the change, then copies the previous values out.
An output-copy failure can therefore return
.Er EFAULT
after changing the limits.
The previous values are copied out after the change is attempted, so a
valid old-limit buffer receives them when the change is refused too; a
failure to read the new limits, to find the target or in the permission
check returns before the buffer is written.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These functions return 0 on success, or -1 on failure and set
.Va errno .
The raw kernel calls return a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/limitprobe.c
sets the descriptor limit to eight, checks that open returns
.Er EMFILE
when those descriptors are occupied, and checks that dup2 refuses a
destination at or above eight with
.Er EBADF .
It also checks process and address-space limits, inheritance, and the
privilege required to raise a hard limit.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EINVAL
.It Er EFAULT
A required limit buffer cannot be read or written.
.It Er EINVAL
For a stored resource, the new soft limit exceeds the new hard limit.
.It Er EPERM
A caller without effective user ID 0 tries to raise a stored hard limit
or select a process with a different real user ID.
The requested descriptor hard limit exceeds 1024.
Selecting a process record that is unused or a zombie also gives this
error.
.It Er ESRCH
No process record exists for
.Fa pid .
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr brk 2 ,
.Xr dup 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr mmap 2 ,
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr limits 7
